{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>לָמַס וַעֲבָדוּךָ</b> (that they become tribute and serve you): until they accept upon themselves tribute and servitude — this is the language of Rabbi Solomon. And in the Sifrei (Shofetim 20): they said, we accept upon ourselves tribute but not servitude, servitude but not tribute — we do not listen to them, until they accept upon themselves both this and that. And the meaning of the tribute (מִסִּים) is that the king of Israel or the Sanhedrin may impose upon them a levy to build a house for the king and his storehouse cities, and likewise for the building of the House of HaShem. And the servitude (שִׁעְבּוּד) is that any man of Israel may take from them to hew his wood and draw his water, and he gives them the appropriate wage. And this is the meaning of <b>לְמַס עוֹבֵד</b> (a tributary servant) written concerning Solomon (I Kings 9:21), that they accepted upon themselves tribute and servitude according to the law of the Torah, not in the manner written (Judges 1:28) וַיְהִי כִּי חָזַק יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיָּשֶׂם אֶת הַכְּנַעֲנִי לָמַס (and it came to pass when Israel grew strong, they set the Canaanite to tribute), for there Scripture does not mention servitude, but rather it is like a king who gives tribute to a king mightier than himself so that he not wage war against him. \n\nNow know that the matter of the men of Gibeon was because they did not know the law of Israel regarding the proclamation of peace, and they acted before the proclamation of peace came to them from Joshua, and therefore they said (Joshua 9:24) וַנִּירָא מְאֹד לְנַפְשׁוֹתֵינוּ (and we were greatly afraid for our lives). Or perhaps they did not wish at first to heed the words of Joshua, but in the end they feared and made themselves out to be foreigners, as it is written (ibid. vv. 3–4): וְיֹשְׁבֵי גִבְעוֹן שָׁמְעוּ אֵת אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה יְהוֹשֻׁעַ לִירִיחוֹ וְלָעָי וַיַּעֲשׂוּ גַם הֵם בְּעָרְמָה (the inhabitants of Gibeon heard what Joshua did to Jericho and Ai, and they also acted with cunning). And it still profited them that they would not become slaves to them but would be allies with them, and because of this the princes were indignant with them, and they would have been killed had it not been for the oath of the princes, because they were obligated to accept tribute and servitude as we have said, yet they cut a covenant with them to be equals to them and allies, helping one another in their wars. And Israel made peace with them because they supposed they were cities very far from the peoples, who had no intention of coming upon them at all. Therefore Joshua cursed them and said (ibid. v. 23) וְעַתָּה אֲרוּרִים אַתֶּם (and now you are cursed), for they were among the cursed peoples whom HaShem cursed, and he did to them as was fitting to be done with them, to fulfill what is stated לָמַס וַעֲבָדוּךָ (tribute and servitude), that they be hewers of wood and drawers of water for the congregation and for the altar of HaShem — and that is the tribute and the servitude as we have explained. \n\nAnd there are those who say that the meaning of וְהָיָה אִם שָׁלוֹם תַּעַנְךָ (and it shall be if they answer you in peace) is at the time of the proclamation, but if they refuse at the outset, we no longer accept them. And the Gibeonites refused at the proclamation, and it would not have been lawful to accept them. \n\nAnd the Rav, Rabbi Moses, wrote (Mishneh Torah, Laws of Kings 6:5) that the princes were indignant with them because they cut a covenant with them, for Scripture says (Exodus 23:32) לֹא תִכְרֹת לָהֶם וְלֵאלֹהֵיהֶם בְּרִית (you shall not cut a covenant with them and with their gods). And this is not correct in my eyes, for the Gibeonites certainly accepted upon themselves not to worship idolatry, as they said (Joshua 9:9) בָּאוּ עֲבָדֶיךָ לְשֵׁם ה' אֱלֹהֶיךָ (your servants have come for the sake of the Name of YHWH your Elohim), and therefore Joshua was not required afterward to tell them to accept upon themselves to worship HaShem, since they were permitted in that covenant just as they were permitted to dwell in the land — for both of these were prohibited only before repentance, as He said (Exodus 23:33) פֶּן יַחֲטִיאוּ אֹתְךָ לִי (lest they cause you to sin against Me), and likewise He said לֹא תִכְרֹת לָהֶם וְלֵאלֹהֵיהֶם בְּרִית. Rather, the matter is as we have said."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Rashi (referred to as 'Rabbi Solomon') and Maimonides (referred to as 'the Rav, Rabbi Moses'); all names preserved as in the original. The phrase פרוסדיתגמא appears as 'proclamation' following the context of the summons to peace in Deut. 20:10-11; the term is a Greek loanword in rabbinic Hebrew whose exact technical sense in this passage is debated."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:52:39.544426445Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}